5. POWER DECAY — FORMAL DEFINITION


Core Principle


Any accumulated influence, wealth, or authority
must gradually decay to prevent concentration and systemic
unfairness, while maintaining stability.


Key points:


Applies to all entities: humans,
	organizations, algorithms
Linear or exponential decay is possible
Can be mitigated only by ongoing contribution
	or systemic need
Preserves survival and sufficiency, only surplus decays




1. WHAT COUNTS AS POWER


We define three primary dimensions:


	
	
	
		
			Dimension

			
			Definition

			
		
	
	
		
			Wealth
				(W)

			
			Resources
				above sufficiency (money, material goods)

			
		
		
			Influence
				(I)

			
			Ability to
				affect systemic outcomes (decisions, votes, social leverage)

			
		
		
			Authority
				(A)

			
			Algorithmic
				or institutional decision-making power

			
		
	




2. DECAY FUNCTION


Exponential decay


P(t+1) = P(t) * (1 - decay_rate)


decay_rate ∈ [0.01, 0.05] per
	cycle (tunable)
Ensures high power naturally reduces without intervention


Linear decay (optional)


P(t+1) = max(P(t) - decay_amount, 0)


Useful if incremental, predictable reductions are preferred




3. CONTRIBUTION OFFSET


Power decay can be mitigated temporarily by
beneficial activity:


P(t+1) = P(t) * (1 - decay_rate) + ContributionBoost


ContributionBoost derived from
	Contribution Index (CI)
Only mitigates decay, cannot exceed sufficiency constraints
Prevents long-term accumulation while rewarding active
	participation




4. RESOURCE-BASED WEALTH DECAY


Wealth above sufficiency triggers automatic
redistribution:


Surplus = max(0, resources[r] - sufficiency[r])
DecayAmount = Surplus * decay_rate


Decayed resources feed system deficit or basic income
	pool
Ensures zero net harm due to hoarding




5. INFLUENCE AND AUTHORITY DECAY


Influence and authority follow similar decay rules:


Influence tied to CI and power above sufficiency
Algorithmic authority decays if:

	
Outcomes concentrate harm
Overrule thresholds exceed fairness bounds

	
Decay ensures all actors are accountable,
	even algorithms




6. CODE — POWER DECAY MODULE


@dataclass
class Power:
    wealth: float
    influence: float
    authority: float
    decay_rate: float = 0.02  # 2% per cycle

def apply_decay(power: Power, contribution_boost: float = 0.0):
    power.wealth *= (1 - power.decay_rate)
    power.influence *= (1 - power.decay_rate)
    power.authority *= (1 - power.decay_rate)
    
    # Mitigate decay with contribution
    power.wealth += contribution_boost * 0.5  # partial offset
    power.influence += contribution_boost * 0.3
    power.authority += contribution_boost * 0.2

    # Enforce non-negative
    power.wealth = max(0, power.wealth)
    power.influence = max(0, power.influence)
    power.authority = max(0, power.authority)




7. SYSTEMIC INTEGRATION


Loop per time cycle:


Update CI based on actions
Update expected harm & justice interventions
Apply power decay to all entities and algorithms
Redistribute decayed surplus to deficits or basic income pool
Recalculate resource sufficiency and fairness metrics


This creates a self-correcting ecosystem:


Contribution is rewarded temporarily
Harm and hoarding are continuously suppressed
Stability is preserved
No permanent elites




8. EDGE CASES


Sudden large contributions → temporary
	surge, decays naturally
Inactive entities → influence &
	surplus decay to sufficiency floor
Algorithms misbehaving → authority decays
	automatically, eventually deprecating them








